Terms used on this site. On other pages, the first mention of each term is underlined with dots: hover for the definition, or click to come here.

## 1991–2020 normals and medians {#normals}

NOAA's 30-year average for each calendar day at the Flagstaff airport (1991–2020 Climate Normals). SNOTEL comparisons use the NRCS 1991–2020 median for the date.

## 250-hPa wind and the jet stream {#jet}

Wind speed at the 250-hPa level, about 10–11 km above sea level, near the level of the jet stream (a narrow band of strong westerly wind). Maps contour 40, 55 and 70 m s⁻¹.

## 500-hPa height {#z500}

The altitude of the 500-hPa pressure surface (hPa = hectopascal = millibar), about 5.5–6 km above sea level. Low heights mark troughs and lows aloft, and the wind at this level blows nearly along the height contours.

## ALERT gauge network {#alert}

Automated Local Evaluation in Real Time: flood-warning rain gauges operated by the City of Flagstaff and Coconino County. Each gauge is a 1-mm tipping bucket that reports every tip by radio.

## Atmospheric river {#atmospheric-river}

A long, narrow corridor of strong water-vapour transport. On this site, 'atmospheric-river strength' means IVT ≥ 250 kg m⁻¹ s⁻¹, a threshold widely used for western North America (e.g. Rutz et al. 2014).

## Closed low {#closed-low}

A low aloft enclosed by at least one height contour. Here: a local minimum of 500-hPa height over a 5° neighbourhood within 1,500 km of Flagstaff.

## ERA5 {#era5}

The fifth-generation ECMWF global atmospheric reanalysis from the Copernicus Climate Change Service: a consistent reconstruction of past weather from 1940 on, combining observations with a forecast model. Used here at 0.25° and 6-hourly; it becomes available about 5 days after real time.

## Event window {#event-window}

The UTC period chosen to contain an event's rain. Gauge totals are summed over it; airport and SNOTEL values use the local (MST) calendar days it covers.

## Integrated vapour transport (IVT) {#ivt}

The horizontal flux of water vapour summed through the depth of the atmosphere, in kg m⁻¹ s⁻¹: how much moisture the wind carries across each metre of a line on the ground, per second.

## MST and UTC {#mst}

Mountain Standard Time is UTC−7. Arizona does not observe daylight saving time, so Flagstaff is on MST all year. ERA5 and maps use UTC (Coordinated Universal Time).

## Network median and percentiles {#network-median}

Statistics across all gauges in the network. The median is the middle gauge value; the 10th–90th percentile range spans the middle 80% of gauges, so a single gauge does not stand in for the city.

## Network-mean rain {#network-mean}

The average of all gauges' hourly rain. Used to time an event: the 'central 80%' is the period between 10% and 90% of the network-mean total.

## NHC best track {#best-track}

The National Hurricane Center's record of each storm's position and intensity every 6 hours. This site uses the operational files; the final best track is released after the season and may differ slightly.

## Provisional and final pages {#provisional}

An event page is provisional until ERA5 covers the whole event window (about 5 days after it ends); it is then rebuilt with ERA5 maps and the atmospheric summary and marked final.

## Remnant disturbance / remnant low {#remnant}

What is left of a tropical cyclone after it loses its tropical structure; NHC continues to track it as a disturbance (DB) or low (LO). Its moisture can still reach Arizona.

## Shaded relief, 3DEP and NHD {#shaded-relief}

Gauge maps show terrain as shaded relief computed from the USGS 3D Elevation Program (3DEP) digital elevation model, with 200-m contours, streams from the USGS National Hydrography Dataset (NHD) and highways from Census TIGER.

## SNOTEL {#snotel}

Snow Telemetry: automated USDA NRCS mountain stations measuring snow water equivalent, snow depth, accumulated precipitation and air temperature. Sites used here: Fort Valley, Snowslide Canyon and Fry.

## Snow water equivalent (SWE) {#swe}

The depth of water that would result if the snowpack melted, in inches.

## Tipping bucket {#tipping-bucket}

A rain gauge that funnels rain into a small two-sided bucket that tips each time it fills (1 mm here). Tips are counted and time-stamped; very intense rain can be undercounted and snow is not measured until it melts.

## Total column water vapour {#tcwv}

The mass of water vapour above a unit area from the ground to the top of the atmosphere, in mm (kg m⁻²); also called precipitable water.

## Tropical cyclone {#tropical-cyclone}

A warm-core low-pressure system that forms over tropical oceans. NHC categories: tropical depression (sustained wind < 34 kt), tropical storm (34–63 kt), hurricane (≥ 64 kt).

## Trough axis {#trough-axis}

The line through the lowest heights of an elongated region of low 500-hPa height. Here: the longitude of the lowest 500-hPa height along Flagstaff's latitude between 135°W and Flagstaff.

## Water year {#water-year}

October 1 to September 30, named for the calendar year in which it ends (water year 2027 began October 1, 2026).
